 ### Non-Square Bounding Boxes
-Allowing the axis-aligned bounding box of a projected 2D Gaussian to have different width and height dimensions, rather than forcing a square shape [S2]. On the Bicycle scene at native resolution with 16x16 tiles, non-square boxes reduced duplicated Gaussians from 55,648,264 (square) to 18,466,556 [S2]. This variant is inherently supported by the adaptive bounding box computation as it computes $rx_f$ and $ry_f$ independently from the covariance matrix diagonal elements.
+Allowing the axis-aligned bounding box of a projected 2D Gaussian to have different width and height dimensions, rather than forcing a square shape [S2]. On the Bicycle scene at native resolution with 16x16 tiles, non-square boxes reduced duplicated Gaussians from 55,648,264 (square) to 18,466,556 [S2]. This variant is inherently supported by the adaptive bounding box computation as it computes $rx_f$ and $ry_f$ independently from the covariance matrix diagonal elements [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per Gaussian. The computation involves a constant number of arithmetic operations, logarithms, and square roots.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory. No additional buffers are required for the bounding box computation itself.
-- **Measured Performance:** On the Bicycle scene at native resolution, combining this bounding box method with other optimizations decreased render time by a factor of 3.77 and GPU memory usage by a factor of 2.11 [S2]. The reduction in duplicated Gaussians from 55.6 million to 18.5 million (with non-square boxes) demonstrates significant reduction in overdraw [S2].
-- **Strengths:** Significantly reduces false-positive tile intersections compared to circular bounding boxes, leading to fewer Gaussians processed per tile and lower memory bandwidth usage [S1, unofficial][S2].
-- **Limitations:** Still produces axis-aligned boxes, which may include empty space for highly rotated Gaussians. The elliptical variants offer tighter bounds but require more complex intersection tests [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Standard rasterization pipelines where tile-based rendering is used and false positives are a bottleneck.
-- **When not to use:** If the Gaussians are highly rotated and the overhead of elliptical intersection tests is justified by the reduction in overdraw, consider the elliptical variants.
